          "source": "thm-weak-maximum-principle-for-coercive-divergence-form-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice through the Poincare and Sobolev suppliers below. Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded $C^1$ domain, and let $L$, $a$ and the real coefficient functions $a^{ij},b^i,c$ be as in [[def-weak-subsolution-and-supersolution-of-a-divergence-form-equation]], with ellipticity constant $\\theta$ and bounds $M_a,M_b,M_c$. Let $f\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and $u\\in H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ be a real local weak subsolution of $Lu=f$. Suppose the weak sign condition\n$$\\int_\\Omega\\bigl(c\\,\\zeta+b^iD_i\\zeta\\bigr)dx\\ge0\\qquad\\text{for every }\\zeta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega),\\ \\zeta\\ge0,$$\nholds, and assume $c\\ge0$ a.e. on $\\Omega$. Then:\n\n1. **Homogeneous case.** If $f=0$ a.e., then\n$$\\operatorname{ess\\,sup}_{\\Omega}u\\le\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^+,$$\nwith the boundary supremum of [[def-weak-subsolution-and-supersolution-of-a-divergence-form-equation]]. If in addition $b\\equiv0$, $c\\equiv0$, and $u$ is a weak solution of $Lu=0$, then $\\operatorname{ess\\,sup}_\\Omega u=\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u$.\n\n2. **Forcing with signed lower order.** If $b\\equiv0$, $c\\ge0$ a.e. and $f\\in L^q(\\Omega)$ for some $q>n/2$ ($q>1$ when $n=2$), then the local inequality extends to all nonnegative $H^1_0(\\Omega)$ tests and\n$$\\operatorname{ess\\,sup}_{\\Omega}u\\le\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^++C\\|f^+\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)},\\qquad C=C(n,q,\\theta,M_a,M_c,\\Omega),$$\nwhere $\\Omega$ enters $C$ only through its Poincare constant and volume.\n\nIf $u$ is a weak supersolution of $Lu=f$ under either set of hypotheses, apply the corresponding bound to $-u$ for the **same** operator coefficients $(a,b,c)$ and source $-f$. This gives $\\operatorname{ess\\,inf}_\\Omega u\\ge-\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^-$ in the homogeneous case and $\\operatorname{ess\\,inf}_\\Omega u\\ge-\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^- -C\\|f^-\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}$ in the forcing case. The maximum-principle conclusions concern real-valued classes and real coefficients.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-weak-global-w-two-p-dirichlet-regularity-for-the-laplacian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$, $n<p<\\infty$, and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded $C^{2,\\alpha}$ domain for some $0<\\alpha<1$. If $u\\in H^1_0(\\Omega)$ is a weak solution of $-\\Delta u=f$ with $f\\in L^p(\\Omega)$, then $u\\in W^{2,p}(\\Omega)\\cap W^{1,p}_0(\\Omega)$ and\n$$\\|u\\|_{W^{2,p}(\\Omega)}\\le C\\bigl(\\|f\\|_{L^p(\\Omega)}+\\|u\\|_{L^p(\\Omega)}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $C=C(n,p,\\Omega)$. This is a weak-to-strong regularity theorem; the estimate applies to the weak solution only after its $W^{2,p}$ membership has been established, and the assumption $p>n$ is the range in which the bootstrap of Sobolev exponents terminates at $p$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $k\\ge1$, $1\\le p\\le\\infty$,\n$\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a\nbounded $C^k$ domain in the graph sense of\n[[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]. Then for every open set\n$V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\overline\\Omega\\subseteq V$ there is a bounded\nlinear extension operator\n$$E:W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)\\longrightarrow W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K),\\qquad (Eu)|_\\Omega=u\\ \\text{a.e. on }\\Omega,$$\nsuch that $\\operatorname{supp}(Eu)$ is a compact subset of $V$ for every\n$u\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. In particular each bounded $C^k$ domain is a\n$W^{k,p}$-extension domain, with an operator whose output is supported in any\nprescribed neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega$. For $k=0$, extension by zero\nis an isometric $L^p$ extension on any open $\\Omega$; its output is supported\nin the compact set $\\overline\\Omega\\subseteq V$ when $\\Omega$ is bounded.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega$ be a bounded $W^{k,p}$-extension domain, $k\\ge1$ and $1\\le p<\\infty$. Let $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and $u\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. Then:\n\n1. if $kp<n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every $q$ with $\\frac1q\\ge\\frac1p-\\frac kn$ (equivalently $1\\le q\\le\\frac{np}{n-kp}$);\n2. if $kp=n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C(q)\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every finite $q$;\n3. if $kp>n$, then for every integer $m\\ge0$ and every $0<\\alpha<1$ with $m+\\alpha<k-\\frac np$ there is a representative in $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ with $\\|u\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$; when $k-\\frac np\\notin\\mathbb Z$ one may take $m=\\lfloor k-\\frac np\\rfloor$ and $\\alpha=k-\\frac np-m$.\n\nHere $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ uses the continuous derivatives of the constructed representative on an ambient neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega$, with norm\n$$\\|g\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}:=\\sum_{|\\beta|\\le m}\\sup_{\\overline\\Omega}|D^\\beta g|+\\sum_{|\\beta|=m}\\sup_{\\substack{x,y\\in\\overline\\Omega\\\\x\\ne y}}\\frac{|D^\\beta g(x)-D^\\beta g(y)|}{|x-y|^\\alpha}.$$\nFor $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ set these norm values to $0$. The proof supplies such an ambient representative, so no boundary differentiability or regularity of $\\partial\\Omega$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-global-schauder-regularity-for-the-weak-dirichlet-laplacian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice together with Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$,\n$0<\\alpha<1$, let $\\Omega$ be a bounded $C^{2,\\alpha}$ domain, let\n$f\\in C^{0,\\alpha}(\\bar\\Omega)$ and $g\\in C^{2,\\alpha}(\\bar\\Omega)$. Then\nthe weak Dirichlet problem $-\\Delta u=f$ in $\\Omega$, $u=g$ on\n$\\partial\\Omega$ (understood as $u-g\\in H^1_0(\\Omega)$) has exactly one\nsolution $u\\in H^1(\\Omega)$, and this solution belongs to\n$C^{2,\\alpha}(\\bar\\Omega)$, satisfies $-\\Delta u=f$ pointwise in $\\Omega$\nand $u=g$ on $\\partial\\Omega$, and obeys\n$$\\|u\\|_{C^{2,\\alpha}(\\bar\\Omega)}\\le C\\bigl(\\|f\\|_{C^{0,\\alpha}(\\bar\\Omega)}+\\|g\\|_{C^{2,\\alpha}(\\bar\\Omega)}\\bigr)$$\nwith $C=C(n,\\alpha,\\Omega)$. This supplies the Laplace base point of the\ncontinuity method rather than assuming it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-interior-w-two-p-estimate-for-uniformly-elliptic-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$, $1<p<\\infty$, $R>0$, $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let $L=a^{ij}\\partial_i\\partial_j+b^i\\partial_i+c$ be uniformly elliptic on $B_R(x_0)$ with constants $\\lambda,\\Lambda$, continuous principal coefficients on the closed ball, and $\\|b\\|_\\infty+\\|c\\|_\\infty\\le M$. Then every $u\\in W^{2,p}(B_R(x_0))$ with $Lu=f\\in L^p$ satisfies the scale-invariant estimate\n$$\\sum_{j=0}^{2}R^{j-2}\\max_{|\\beta|=j}\\|D^\\beta u\\|_{L^p(B_{R/2}(x_0))}\\le C\\bigl(R^{-2}\\|u\\|_{L^p(B_R(x_0))}+\\|f\\|_{L^p(B_R(x_0))}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $D^0u=u$ and the maximum runs over multi-indices of order $j$,\nwhere $C$ may depend on $n,p,\\lambda,\\Lambda,R\\|b\\|_\\infty,R^2\\|c\\|_\\infty$ and the modulus of continuity of $A$ on the ball. A radius-independent constant requires uniform control of these dimensionless lower-order bounds and of the modulus. The theorem assumes continuity of $A$; no estimate for merely measurable principal coefficients is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-interior-schauder-estimate-for-uniformly-elliptic-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$, $0<\\alpha<1$, $R>0$, $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let $L=a^{ij}\\partial_i\\partial_j+b^i\\partial_i+c$ be uniformly elliptic on $B_R(x_0)$ with constants $\\lambda,\\Lambda$, $[A]_{0,\\alpha;B_R(x_0)}\\le K$, and $b,c\\in C^{0,\\alpha}(B_R(x_0))$ with finite global Hölder seminorms. Let $u\\in C^2(B_R(x_0))\\cap L^\\infty(B_R(x_0))$ satisfy $Lu=f$ pointwise with $f\\in C^{0,\\alpha}(B_R(x_0))$. Then $u\\in C^{2,\\alpha}(B_{R/2}(x_0))$ and\n$$\\|u\\|^{*}_{2,\\alpha;B_{R/2}(x_0)}\\le C\\bigl(\\|u\\|_{\\infty;B_R(x_0)}+R^2\\|f\\|_{\\infty;B_R(x_0)}+R^{2+\\alpha}[f]_{0,\\alpha;B_R(x_0)}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $C$ depends only on $n,\\alpha,\\lambda,\\Lambda$ and the finite dimensionless coefficient bounds $R^\\alpha K+R\\|b\\|_\\infty+R^{1+\\alpha}[b]_{0,\\alpha}+R^2\\|c\\|_\\infty+R^{2+\\alpha}[c]_{0,\\alpha}$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-w-one-two-is-a-hilbert-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, $n\\ge1$, and $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$. On $H^1(\\Omega)=W^{1,2}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ ([[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], [[def-hk-and-hk-zero-notation]]) define $$(u,v)_{H^1}:=(u,v)_{L^2}+\\sum_{i=1}^n(D_iu,D_iv)_{L^2},$$ with the $L^2$ inner product of [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. Then $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_{H^1}$ is an inner product on the Sobolev classes whose induced norm is the $W^{1,2}$ norm of [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]], and $H^1(\\Omega)$ is a Hilbert space for it. The zero-boundary space $H^1_0(\\Omega)$ is a closed subspace of $H^1(\\Omega)$ ([[def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure]]) and hence a Hilbert space for the restricted inner product. The pairing is linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the second, in the convention of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f,g\\in\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$, then\n\n$$\\int |fg|\\,d\\mu\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|g\\|_2.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $f,g$ is zero almost everywhere,\nor there is a constant $c>0$ with\n\n$$|f|^2=c\\,|g|^2\\qquad\\mu\\text{-almost everywhere}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f\\in L^1(\\mu)$, then\n$$\\left|\\int f\\,d\\mu\\right|\\le\\int |f|\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. Let $f_n:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable for every $n\\ge1$, with $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$, and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$ for every $x\\in X$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "thm-general-stokes-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M$ be an oriented smooth $n$-manifold with boundary, $n\\geq1$, and let $\\eta\\in\\Omega_c^{n-1}(M)$. With $j:\\partial M\\hookrightarrow M$ and the outward-normal-first orientation,\n$$\\int_M d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial M}j^*\\eta.$$\nAn empty boundary contributes zero; in dimension one its integral is a finite signed sum of point values.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "First, $\\log|z|^2$ is locally integrable and harmonic off zero. For a smooth compactly supported test function $\\varphi$, apply [F14] on an annulus excluding $|z|\\le\\varepsilon$ to the one-form $\\log|z|^2*d\\varphi-\\varphi*d\\log|z|^2$. Its inner-boundary contribution is $-\\varepsilon\\log(\\varepsilon^2)\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\partial_r\\varphi(\\varepsilon,\\theta)d\\theta+2\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\varphi(\\varepsilon,\\theta)d\\theta$, which tends to $4\\pi\\varphi(0)$; the outer contribution is zero. Thus $\\Delta\\log|z-z_j|^2=4\\pi\\delta_{z_j}$ by translation. For $z\\ne z_j$, put $t_j=a/(|z-z_j|^2+a)$. Then $0<t_j<1$, $e^{u_-}=\\prod_j(1-t_j)^{m_j}$, and direct radial differentiation gives $\\Delta u_-=-\\frac4a\\sum_jm_jt_j^2+4\\pi\\sum_jm_j\\delta_{z_j}$ distributionally. If $N>0$, let $t_*:=\\max_jt_j$; then $1-e^{u_-}\\ge t_*$ and $\\sum_jm_jt_j^2\\le Nt_*$. Hence $\\Delta u_-+\\kappa(1-e^{u_-})-4\\pi D\\ge(\\kappa-4N/a)t_*\\ge0$. For $N=0$, $u_-=0$ and the subsolution inequality is equality.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F14",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "The denominator $R^2-\\overline z_jz$ has no zeros on $\\overline D_R$ because $|z_j|<R$. The logarithmic fundamental-solution identity and the boundary equality $R^2|z-z_j|^2=|R^2-\\overline z_jz|^2$ for $|z|=R$ give $\\Delta u_{s,R}=4\\pi D$ and $u_{s,R}=0$ on $\\partial D_R$. Consequently $A_R=e^{u_{s,R}}$ is smooth and nonnegative on $\\overline D_R$, vanishes at the centers, and equals one on the boundary. Since $\\Delta\\psi_R=-\\kappa$, the supersolution residual away from the Dirac masses is $\\Delta\\psi_R+\\kappa(1-A_Re^{\\psi_R})=-\\kappa A_Re^{\\psi_R}\\le0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "By [F10], $H^1_0(D_R;\\mathbb R)$ is a real Hilbert space with the full $W^{1,2}$ inner product; [F2] gives its norm. For any $L_R>0$, [F11] and the pointwise Euclidean inequality $|\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla w|\\le|\\nabla v||\\nabla w|$ give $|a(v,w)|\\le(1+L_R)\\|v\\|_{W^{1,2}}\\|w\\|_{W^{1,2}}$, so the form is bounded. Also $a(v,v)\\ge\\min\\{1,L_R\\}\\|v\\|_{W^{1,2}}^2$, so it is coercive. For $f\\in L^2(D_R)$, [F11] gives $\\int|fw|\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|w\\|_2$, and [F12] then gives $|\\int fw|\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|w\\|_2\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|w\\|_{W^{1,2}}$. Thus [F3] gives a unique zero-Dirichlet weak solution of $(-\\Delta+L_R)u=f$. The resolver is linear by uniqueness and positivity preserving: if $f\\ge0$, then $-u$ is a weak subsolution of $(-\\Delta+L_R)(-u)=-f\\le0$ with zero trace, so [F4] gives $u\\ge0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F10",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The lower and upper barriers have the same logarithmic singular part, so $w=u_--u_{s,R}-\\psi_R$ extends continuously across every center. Their subsolution and supersolution inequalities imply $\\Delta w-c_Rw\\ge0$, where $c_R=\\kappa\\int_0^1\\exp((1-t)(u_{s,R}+\\psi_R)+tu_-)\\,dt\\ge0$, extended continuously at the centers. On $\\partial D_R$, $w=u_-<0$ (and $w=0$ when $D$ is empty). Applying [F4] to $(-\\Delta+c_R)w\\le0$ with nonpositive boundary trace gives $w\\le0$, hence $v_0=u_--u_{s,R}\\le\\psi_R$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Set $G_R(v)=\\kappa+L_Rv-\\kappa A_Re^v$ and choose $L_R\\ge\\kappa\\max_{\\overline D_R}(A_Re^{\\psi_R})$. For $v\\le\\psi_R$, $G_R'(v)=L_R-\\kappa A_Re^v\\ge0$, so the iteration map $T_R=(-\\Delta+L_R)^{-1}G_R$ is order preserving. The subsolution inequality from step 2.1 gives $v_0\\le T_Rv_0$ and the supersolution inequality from step 1.2 gives $T_R\\psi_R\\le\\psi_R$, by positivity of the resolver in step 1.3. Thus $v_0\\le v_1\\le\\cdots\\le\\psi_R$; every iterate after $v_0$ has zero Dirichlet trace. Each has a smooth representative: starting from smooth $v_0$ and $A_R$, rewrite its shifted resolvent equation as a Laplace equation, use boundedness and [F5] for $W^{2,p}$, then [F6]–[F7] and local bootstrapping; this also identifies continuous representatives up to the boundary. These representatives realize the order pointwise.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The monotone iterates have a bounded pointwise limit $v_R$ on the finite disk. If $C\\ge\\sup_{D_R}(\\psi_R-v_0)^2$, then $0\\le C-(v_R-v_k)^2\\uparrow C$. Applying [F13] and subtracting the finite integral of C gives $\\int_{D_R}(v_R-v_k)^2\\to0$, so $v_k\\to v_R$ in $L^2(D_R)$. For functions $v,w$ with $v_0\\le v,w\\le\\psi_R$, the pointwise mean-value formula gives $|e^v-e^w|\\le e^{\\psi_R}|v-w|$; hence $\\|G_R(v)-G_R(w)\\|_2\\le(L_R+\\kappa\\|A_R\\|_\\infty e^{\\max\\psi_R})\\|v-w\\|_2$. Subtract the two resolvent equations for $T_Rv,T_Rw$ and test with $u=T_Rv-T_Rw$. The form in step 1.3 satisfies $a(u,u)\\ge\\min\\{1,L_R\\}\\|u\\|_{W^{1,2}}^2$, while [F11] and [F12] bound the right side by $\\|G_R(v)-G_R(w)\\|_2\\|u\\|_{W^{1,2}}$; division when $u\\ne0$ (and trivially when $u=0$) gives $\\|T_Rv-T_Rw\\|_{W^{1,2}}\\le\\min\\{1,L_R\\}^{-1}\\|G_R(v)-G_R(w)\\|_2$. Therefore $T_Rv_k\\to T_Rv_R$ in $H^1_0$; the shifted sequence $v_{k+1}=T_Rv_k$ has the same $L^2$ limit as $v_k$, so uniqueness of an $L^2$ limit gives $v_R=T_Rv_R$. It is a weak solution of $-\\Delta v_R=\\kappa(1-A_Re^{v_R})$ with zero trace.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Since $v_0\\le v_R\\le\\psi_R$ and $A_R$ is bounded on $\\overline D_R$, the right-hand side in step 4.1 lies in $L^p(D_R)$ for every finite $p$. For a prescribed $0<\\alpha<1$, choose $p>2/(1-\\alpha)$. The disk is bounded and smooth, so [F5] gives $v_R\\in W^{2,p}$; [F6] gives a $C^{1,\\beta}$ representative with $\\beta>\\alpha$ and zero boundary values. The nonlinear right-hand side is then $C^{0,\\alpha}$, and [F7] upgrades the weak solution to $C^{2,\\alpha}(\\overline D_R)$. Repeated local Schauder bootstrapping, using [F9], makes $v_R$ smooth in $D_R$. Setting $u_R=u_{s,R}+v_R$ proves its distributional equation and logarithmic center behavior.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "If $u_R$ were positive somewhere, its boundary value is zero and it tends to $-\\infty$ at each center, so it would attain a positive interior maximum away from the centers. There $\\Delta u_R=\\kappa(e^{u_R}-1)>0$ by its equation, contradicting the second-derivative test at a maximum. Thus $u_R\\le0$; together with step 2.1 this gives $u_-\\le u_R\\le0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "Fix a disk $U=B_S(0)$ containing the centers in its interior and let $s=2\\sum_jm_j\\log|z-z_j|$. For every sufficiently large $R$, $w_R=u_R-s$ is smooth on $\\overline U$. On $\\partial U$, steps 1.1 and 6.1 give the uniform bounds $u_--s\\le w_R\\le-s$, whose endpoints are finite there. On $U$, $\\Delta w_R=\\kappa(e^s e^{w_R}-1)=\\kappa(e^{u_R}-1)\\in[-\\kappa,0]$. A quadratic barrier $Q(z)=M+\\kappa(S^2-|z|^2)/4$, with $M$ larger than the uniform boundary bound, satisfies $\\Delta(w_R-Q)\\ge0$ and $\\Delta(-w_R-Q)\\ge0$, and both differences are nonpositive on $\\partial U$; the disk maximum principle [F4], with zero lower-order coefficient, yields $|w_R|\\le Q$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-8-1",
          "step": "8.1",
          "claim": "On any smaller compact disk $K\\Subset U$, [F8] applied on finitely many nested balls and the uniform $L^\\infty$ bound of step 7.1 yields uniform $W^{2,p}$ bounds for $w_R$ for every finite $p>2$, since its Laplacian is bounded. For a prescribed $0<\\alpha<1$, choose $p>2/(1-\\alpha)$ and then $\\beta$ with $\\alpha<\\beta<1-2/p$; [F6] gives a uniform $C^{1,\\beta}$ bound on a slightly larger compact disk. As $e^s$ is smooth and $\\Delta w_R=\\kappa(e^s e^{w_R}-1)$, the right-hand side has a uniform $C^{0,\\alpha}$ bound there; [F9] then yields a uniform $C^{2,\\alpha}$ bound on $K$. These bounds are independent of $R$ and are the local compactness estimates used for disk exhaustion.",
