    "thm-qftx-taubes-arbitrary-divisor-plane-vortex": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qftx-critical-abelian-higgs-divisor-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $e>0$ and $\\xi>0$ be real parameters, set $\\kappa=2e^2\\xi$, identify the oriented Euclidean plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with $\\mathbb C$ using coordinates $(x_1,x_2)$, and use the Hodge star convention $*dx_1=dx_2$, $*dx_2=-dx_1$. A field pair is a smooth real one-form $A=A_1dx_1+A_2dx_2$ on the trivial complex line over $\\mathbb R^2$ and a smooth function $q:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb C$. Its unit-charge covariant derivative is $D_Aq=dq-iAq$, its curvature is $F_A=dA=B\\,dx_1\\wedge dx_2$ with $B=\\partial_1A_2-\\partial_2A_1$, and its transverse energy is\n$$E(A,q)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{B^2}{2e^2}+|D_{A,1}q|^2+|D_{A,2}q|^2+\\frac{e^2}{2}(|q|^2-\\xi)^2\\right)dx_1dx_2.$$\nThe pair has finite energy when this nonnegative Lebesgue integral is finite. An admissible boundary sector consists of an integer $n$, a radius $R_0$, and smooth functions $\\rho>0$ and real $2\\pi$-periodic $\\phi$ on $r>R_0$ such that $q(r,\\theta)=\\rho(r,\\theta)e^{i(n\\theta+\\phi(r,\\theta))}$ and $\\rho\\to\\sqrt\\xi$, $\\phi\\to0$ uniformly as $r\\to\\infty$; $n$ is its winding. A gauge transformation is $(q,A)\\mapsto(e^{i\\chi}q,A+d\\chi)$ for a smooth real-valued function $\\chi$ on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, the positive critical first-order equations are $(D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$; for $n\\le0$ the negative equations are $(D_{A,1}-iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=-e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$. The adjective critical refers to the displayed energy normalization and these square-saturating equations. For a positive-winding solution, its divisor is an effective finite divisor $D=\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j[z_j]$, where the $z_j\\in\\mathbb C$ are distinct, the multiplicities $m_j$ are positive integers, $q$ vanishes exactly at the $z_j$, and near each $z_j$ it has the local form $q(z)=(z-z_j)^{m_j}g_j(z)$ with $g_j$ smooth and nonzero there. The degree is $N=\\sum_jm_j$; the empty divisor has degree zero. Negative winding uses the conjugate field and reversed orientation, with $N=|n|$.\n\nOn the complement of the divisor, define $h=\\log(|q|^2/\\xi)$. The associated scalar Taubes datum is a real function smooth away from the divisor such that $h-2m_j\\log|z-z_j|$ extends smoothly across every $z_j$, $h\\to0$ at infinity, and, as a distribution,\n$$\\Delta h+\\kappa(1-e^h)=4\\pi\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j\\delta_{z_j},\\qquad \\langle\\delta_z,\\psi\\rangle=\\psi(z)\\quad(\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)).$$\nWhen the limit exists, the magnetic flux is $\\Phi(A)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{|x|<R}B(x)\\,dx_1dx_2$. These are mathematical configuration and normalization conventions; they make no assertion that a physical model describes nature.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.4",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qftx-taubes-disk-barriers-and-monotone-iteration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\kappa>0$ and let $D=\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j[z_j]$ be a finite effective divisor in $\\mathbb C$, where the centers $z_j$ are distinct and $m_j\\in\\mathbb N$; write $N=\\sum_jm_j$. For any $a>0$ with $a\\ge4N/\\kappa$, the function\n$$u_-(z)=\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j\\log\\frac{|z-z_j|^2}{|z-z_j|^2+a}$$\nis a global distributional subsolution of $\\Delta u+\\kappa(1-e^u)=4\\pi\\sum_jm_j\\delta_{z_j}$. For every $R>\\max_j|z_j|$ (with the empty maximum interpreted as $0$), let $D_R=\\{|z|<R\\}$ and define\n$$u_{s,R}(z)=\\sum_jm_j\\log\\frac{R^2|z-z_j|^2}{|R^2-\\overline z_jz|^2},\\qquad \\psi_R(z)=\\frac{\\kappa}{4}(R^2-|z|^2),\\qquad A_R=e^{u_{s,R}}.$$\nThen $u_{s,R}$ is the zero-boundary Dirichlet Green sum with $\\Delta u_{s,R}=4\\pi\\sum_jm_j\\delta_{z_j}$; $u_{s,R}+\\psi_R$ is a supersolution and $u_-\\le u_{s,R}+\\psi_R$ on $D_R$. Write $H^1_0(D_R;\\mathbb R)$ for the closure of $C_c^\\infty(D_R;\\mathbb R)$ in the real Sobolev space $W^{1,2}(D_R;\\mathbb R)$. For any constant $L_R\\ge\\kappa\\max_{\\overline D_R}(A_Re^{\\psi_R})$, the positive zero-Dirichlet resolver $(-\\Delta+L_R)^{-1}$ on $H^1_0(D_R;\\mathbb R)$ defines the monotone iteration\n$$v_0=u_--u_{s,R},\\qquad v_{k+1}=(-\\Delta+L_R)^{-1}\\bigl(\\kappa+L_Rv_k-\\kappa A_Re^{v_k}\\bigr).$$\nIt converges pointwise and in $H^1(D_R)$ to a solution $v_R\\in C^{2,\\alpha}(\\overline D_R)$ for every $0<\\alpha<1$, with $v_0\\le v_R\\le\\psi_R$ and zero boundary trace. Thus $u_R=u_{s,R}+v_R$ solves the distributional divisor equation, satisfies $u_-\\le u_R\\le0$, and has $u_R-2\\sum_jm_j\\log|z-z_j|$ smooth at the centers. For the fixed singular function $s(z)=2\\sum_jm_j\\log|z-z_j|$, every compact $K\\subset\\mathbb C$ and every $p>2$ admit constants independent of sufficiently large $R$ such that $u_R-s$ is uniformly bounded in $W^{2,p}(K)$. For each $0<\\alpha<1$, choosing $p>2/(1-\\alpha)$ also gives a uniform $C^{2,\\alpha}(K)$ bound. These are the local compactness estimates for disk exhaustion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-interior-w-two-p-estimate-for-uniformly-elliptic-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$, $1<p<\\infty$, $R>0$, $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let $L=a^{ij}\\partial_i\\partial_j+b^i\\partial_i+c$ be uniformly elliptic on $B_R(x_0)$ with constants $\\lambda,\\Lambda$, continuous principal coefficients on the closed ball, and $\\|b\\|_\\infty+\\|c\\|_\\infty\\le M$. Then every $u\\in W^{2,p}(B_R(x_0))$ with $Lu=f\\in L^p$ satisfies the scale-invariant estimate\n$$\\sum_{j=0}^{2}R^{j-2}\\max_{|\\beta|=j}\\|D^\\beta u\\|_{L^p(B_{R/2}(x_0))}\\le C\\bigl(R^{-2}\\|u\\|_{L^p(B_R(x_0))}+\\|f\\|_{L^p(B_R(x_0))}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $D^0u=u$ and the maximum runs over multi-indices of order $j$,\nwhere $C$ may depend on $n,p,\\lambda,\\Lambda,R\\|b\\|_\\infty,R^2\\|c\\|_\\infty$ and the modulus of continuity of $A$ on the ball. A radius-independent constant requires uniform control of these dimensionless lower-order bounds and of the modulus. The theorem assumes continuity of $A$; no estimate for merely measurable principal coefficients is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-interior-schauder-estimate-for-uniformly-elliptic-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$, $0<\\alpha<1$, $R>0$, $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let $L=a^{ij}\\partial_i\\partial_j+b^i\\partial_i+c$ be uniformly elliptic on $B_R(x_0)$ with constants $\\lambda,\\Lambda$, $[A]_{0,\\alpha;B_R(x_0)}\\le K$, and $b,c\\in C^{0,\\alpha}(B_R(x_0))$ with finite global Hölder seminorms. Let $u\\in C^2(B_R(x_0))\\cap L^\\infty(B_R(x_0))$ satisfy $Lu=f$ pointwise with $f\\in C^{0,\\alpha}(B_R(x_0))$. Then $u\\in C^{2,\\alpha}(B_{R/2}(x_0))$ and\n$$\\|u\\|^{*}_{2,\\alpha;B_{R/2}(x_0)}\\le C\\bigl(\\|u\\|_{\\infty;B_R(x_0)}+R^2\\|f\\|_{\\infty;B_R(x_0)}+R^{2+\\alpha}[f]_{0,\\alpha;B_R(x_0)}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $C$ depends only on $n,\\alpha,\\lambda,\\Lambda$ and the finite dimensionless coefficient bounds $R^\\alpha K+R\\|b\\|_\\infty+R^{1+\\alpha}[b]_{0,\\alpha}+R^2\\|c\\|_\\infty+R^{2+\\alpha}[c]_{0,\\alpha}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega$ be a bounded $W^{k,p}$-extension domain, $k\\ge1$ and $1\\le p<\\infty$. Let $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and $u\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. Then:\n\n1. if $kp<n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every $q$ with $\\frac1q\\ge\\frac1p-\\frac kn$ (equivalently $1\\le q\\le\\frac{np}{n-kp}$);\n2. if $kp=n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C(q)\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every finite $q$;\n3. if $kp>n$, then for every integer $m\\ge0$ and every $0<\\alpha<1$ with $m+\\alpha<k-\\frac np$ there is a representative in $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ with $\\|u\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$; when $k-\\frac np\\notin\\mathbb Z$ one may take $m=\\lfloor k-\\frac np\\rfloor$ and $\\alpha=k-\\frac np-m$.\n\nHere $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ uses the continuous derivatives of the constructed representative on an ambient neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega$, with norm\n$$\\|g\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}:=\\sum_{|\\beta|\\le m}\\sup_{\\overline\\Omega}|D^\\beta g|+\\sum_{|\\beta|=m}\\sup_{\\substack{x,y\\in\\overline\\Omega\\\\x\\ne y}}\\frac{|D^\\beta g(x)-D^\\beta g(y)|}{|x-y|^\\alpha}.$$\nFor $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ set these norm values to $0$. The proof supplies such an ambient representative, so no boundary differentiability or regularity of $\\partial\\Omega$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $k\\ge1$, $1\\le p\\le\\infty$,\n$\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a\nbounded $C^k$ domain in the graph sense of\n[[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]. Then for every open set\n$V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\overline\\Omega\\subseteq V$ there is a bounded\nlinear extension operator\n$$E:W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)\\longrightarrow W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K),\\qquad (Eu)|_\\Omega=u\\ \\text{a.e. on }\\Omega,$$\nsuch that $\\operatorname{supp}(Eu)$ is a compact subset of $V$ for every\n$u\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. In particular each bounded $C^k$ domain is a\n$W^{k,p}$-extension domain, with an operator whose output is supported in any\nprescribed neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega$. For $k=0$, extension by zero\nis an isometric $L^p$ extension on any open $\\Omega$; its output is supported\nin the compact set $\\overline\\Omega\\subseteq V$ when $\\Omega$ is bounded.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-real-and-euclidean-vector-valued-ascoli-arzela",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Assume the Axiom of Choice.** Let $X$ be a nonempty compact metric space and let $n\\in\\mathbb N$ with $n\\geq1$. For $Z=\\mathbb R$ and for $Z=\\mathbb R^n$ with its Euclidean metric:\n\n1. A family $\\mathcal F\\subseteq C(X,Z)$ is compact in the uniform topology if and only if it is uniformly closed, equicontinuous, and pointwise bounded.\n2. Every pointwise bounded equicontinuous sequence in $C(X,Z)$ has a uniformly convergent subsequence with limit in $C(X,Z)$.\n\nFor real-valued families, equicontinuity is uniform over the compact domain, and equicontinuity together with pointwise boundedness gives one bound for all values. The same one-bound conclusion holds for Euclidean-valued families.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-weak-maximum-principle-for-coercive-divergence-form-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice through the Poincare and Sobolev suppliers below. Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded $C^1$ domain, and let $L$, $a$ and the real coefficient functions $a^{ij},b^i,c$ be as in [[def-weak-subsolution-and-supersolution-of-a-divergence-form-equation]], with ellipticity constant $\\theta$ and bounds $M_a,M_b,M_c$. Let $f\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and $u\\in H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ be a real local weak subsolution of $Lu=f$. Suppose the weak sign condition\n$$\\int_\\Omega\\bigl(c\\,\\zeta+b^iD_i\\zeta\\bigr)dx\\ge0\\qquad\\text{for every }\\zeta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega),\\ \\zeta\\ge0,$$\nholds, and assume $c\\ge0$ a.e. on $\\Omega$. Then:\n\n1. **Homogeneous case.** If $f=0$ a.e., then\n$$\\operatorname{ess\\,sup}_{\\Omega}u\\le\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^+,$$\nwith the boundary supremum of [[def-weak-subsolution-and-supersolution-of-a-divergence-form-equation]]. If in addition $b\\equiv0$, $c\\equiv0$, and $u$ is a weak solution of $Lu=0$, then $\\operatorname{ess\\,sup}_\\Omega u=\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u$.\n\n2. **Forcing with signed lower order.** If $b\\equiv0$, $c\\ge0$ a.e. and $f\\in L^q(\\Omega)$ for some $q>n/2$ ($q>1$ when $n=2$), then the local inequality extends to all nonnegative $H^1_0(\\Omega)$ tests and\n$$\\operatorname{ess\\,sup}_{\\Omega}u\\le\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^++C\\|f^+\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)},\\qquad C=C(n,q,\\theta,M_a,M_c,\\Omega),$$\nwhere $\\Omega$ enters $C$ only through its Poincare constant and volume.\n\nIf $u$ is a weak supersolution of $Lu=f$ under either set of hypotheses, apply the corresponding bound to $-u$ for the **same** operator coefficients $(a,b,c)$ and source $-f$. This gives $\\operatorname{ess\\,inf}_\\Omega u\\ge-\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^-$ in the homogeneous case and $\\operatorname{ess\\,inf}_\\Omega u\\ge-\\sup_{\\partial\\Omega}u^- -C\\|f^-\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}$ in the forcing case. The maximum-principle conclusions concern real-valued classes and real coefficients.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-strong-maximum-principle-for-classical-subharmonic-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice for the mean-inequality input. Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a domain, and let $u\\in C^2(\\Omega)$ satisfy $\\Delta u\\ge0$. If there is $a\\in\\Omega$ with $u(x)\\le u(a)$ for every $x\\in\\Omega$, then $u$ is constant.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Choose radii $R_k\\to\\infty$ with every center in $D_{R_k}$. Let $s=2\\sum_jm_j\\log|z-z_j|$ and $w_k=u_{R_k}-s$. For each compact disk $K$ and each $0<\\alpha<1$, [F2] gives a uniform $C^{2,\\alpha}$ bound on $w_k$ on a slightly larger disk. Thus $(w_k,Dw_k,D^2w_k)$ is uniformly bounded and equicontinuous there. Apply the Euclidean sequence statement [F6] to the finite derivative tuple on each nonempty compact disk. A countable diagonal selection under [A1] then gives locally uniform convergence of $w_k$ and all its derivatives through order two. The fundamental theorem of calculus on coordinate segments identifies the limiting derivative tuple as the first and second derivatives of one $C^2$ function w. Thus the subsequence converges in $C^2$ on every compact disk. Set $h=s+w$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "On every compact set, $e^{u_{R_k}}=e^s e^{w_k}$ converges uniformly to $e^h$. Passing to the limit in the weak divisor equation for $u_{R_k}$ gives $\\Delta h+\\kappa(1-e^h)=4\\pi D$ distributionally. Since $s-2m_j\\log|z-z_j|$ is smooth near each center and $w$ is $C^2$ there, the prescribed logarithmic subtraction extends continuously. The equation for $w$ is $\\Delta w=\\kappa(e^s e^w-1)$ with smooth coefficient $e^s=|P|^2$; repeated interior Schauder regularity [F4] bootstraps $w$ to $C^\\infty$, so each center subtraction is smooth. The bounds $u_-\\le u_{R_k}\\le0$ pass to the limit, and $u_-(z)\\to0$ at infinity; hence $h\\le0$ and $h\\to0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "If $h_1,h_2$ are two solutions with the same divisor and boundary condition, $v=h_1-h_2$ extends smoothly across the centers. Subtracting their equations gives $\\Delta v-c(z)v=0$, where $c(z)=\\kappa\\int_0^1 e^{(1-t)h_2+th_1}\\,dt\\ge0$, extended continuously by $0$ at the centers. On $D_R$, the weak maximum principle [F7] applied to $v$ and $-v$ bounds $|v|$ by $\\sup_{\\partial D_R}|v|$. This boundary supremum tends to $0$ as $R\\to\\infty$, so $v=0$ and the scalar solution is unique. When $D$ is empty, $h=0$ is a solution and the same uniqueness argument shows it is the only one.",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "step": "3.2",
          "claim": "Suppose $N>0$ and put $w=-h\\ge0$. Away from the divisor, $\\Delta w=\\kappa(1-e^{-w})\\le\\kappa w$. On $\\Omega=(\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{z_j\\})\\times(-\\pi/(2\\sqrt\\kappa),\\pi/(2\\sqrt\\kappa))$, define $U(z,t)=-w(z)\\cos(\\sqrt\\kappa t)$. Then $U\\le0$ and $\\Delta_{z,t}U=\\cos(\\sqrt\\kappa t)(\\kappa w-\\Delta w)\\ge0$. The finite-punctured plane is path connected (polygonal paths can detour around finitely many points), hence $\\Omega$ is connected. If $w$ vanished at a point, $U$ would attain its maximum $0$ in $\\Omega$; [F8] would force $U\\equiv0$, so $w\\equiv0$ off the divisor, contradicting $w(z)\\to+\\infty$ at each positive-multiplicity center. Thus $h<0$ away from the divisor.",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-3",
          "step": "3.3",
          "claim": "Fix $0<\\alpha<\\sqrt\\kappa$. Since $w=-h\\to0$, choose $c$ with $\\alpha^2<c<\\kappa$ and $R_0$ so large that $\\kappa(1-e^{-w})\\ge c w$ for $|z|\\ge R_0$. Let $\\varphi(z)=C e^{-\\alpha(|z|-R_0)}$ with $C\\ge\\sup_{|z|=R_0}w$. For $r=|z|\\ge R_0$, $\\Delta\\varphi=(\\alpha^2-\\alpha/r)\\varphi<c\\varphi$, so $(\\Delta-c)(w-\\varphi)\\ge0$. The function $w-\\varphi$ is nonpositive on $|z|=R_0$ and tends to $0$ at infinity. If it were positive somewhere, its positive maximum would be attained at a finite interior point, where $(\\Delta-c)(w-\\varphi)<0$, a contradiction. Therefore $0\\le w\\le\\varphi$, proving the claimed scalar exponential bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-4",
          "step": "3.4",
          "claim": "Let $P(z)=\\prod_j(z-z_j)^{m_j}$ and $s=2\\log|P|$. The factor $h-s$ is smooth on all of $\\mathbb C$. Define $q=\\sqrt\\xi e^{(h-s)/2}P$ and $A=-\\frac12*d(h-s)$; both extend smoothly over the centers and $q$ has exactly the prescribed zeros. On the punctured plane write $P=|P|e^{i\\chi}$; holomorphicity gives $d\\chi=\\frac12*ds$, hence $A=d\\chi-\\frac12*dh$. Writing $q=\\sqrt\\xi e^{h/2}e^{i\\chi}$ and using $*dh=( -\\partial_2h)dx_1+(\\partial_1h)dx_2$ gives $D_{A,1}q=\\frac12e^{i\\chi}\\sqrt\\xi e^{h/2}(\\partial_1h-i\\partial_2h)$ and $D_{A,2}q=\\frac12e^{i\\chi}\\sqrt\\xi e^{h/2}(\\partial_2h+i\\partial_1h)$, so $D_{A,1}q+iD_{A,2}q=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
