        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "The curvature is $dA=-\\frac12\\Delta(h-s)\\,dx_1\\wedge dx_2$. Since $\\Delta s=4\\pi D$ and $h$ satisfies the divisor equation, $\\Delta(h-s)=-\\kappa(1-e^h)$; therefore $B=\\frac\\kappa2(1-e^h)=e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$. On the exterior of a disk, $e^{-i\\chi}$ is a smooth local gauge, not asserted to extend across the zeros; it gives $q_{\\mathrm{ext}}=\\sqrt\\xi e^{h/2}$ and $A_{\\mathrm{ext}}=-\\frac12*dh$. The phase satisfies $\\chi=N\\theta+O(r^{-1})$ because $P(z)=z^N\\prod_j(1-z_j/z)^{m_j}$. Thus the gauge-invariant $B,D_Aq$ decay exponentially by step 4.1, while no exponential decay of the raw phase correction is claimed.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
