        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "The reconstructed fields are smooth on compact sets. In the exterior gauge of step 5.1, $B$, $D_Aq$, and $|q|^2-\\xi=\\xi(e^h-1)$ all decay exponentially, so each squared term in the defining energy is integrable on $\\mathbb R^2$; hence the energy is finite. To compute flux, write $v=h-s$, so $\\Delta v=-\\kappa(1-e^h)$. Since $v$ is smooth at the origin, polar coordinates and the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus give\n$$\\int_{D_R}\\Delta v\\,dx_1dx_2=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^R\\partial_r(r\\partial_rv)\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}R\\partial_rv(R,\\theta)\\,d\\theta,$$\nas $r\\partial_rv\\to0$ at $r=0$. By step 4.1, $\\partial_rh$ decays exponentially; from $s=2N\\log r+O(r^{-1})$, $\\partial_rs=2N/R+O(R^{-2})$ uniformly in $\\theta$. Therefore the boundary integral tends to $-4\\pi N$, so $\\kappa\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}(1-e^h)=4\\pi N$. Using $B=\\frac\\kappa2(1-e^h)$ gives flux $2\\pi N$. For the empty divisor $h=0$, the reconstruction is the flat constant vacuum and has zero flux.",
