    "thm-qftx-critical-vortex-global-energy-minimum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qftx-critical-abelian-higgs-divisor-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $e>0$ and $\\xi>0$ be real parameters, set $\\kappa=2e^2\\xi$, identify the oriented Euclidean plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with $\\mathbb C$ using coordinates $(x_1,x_2)$, and use the Hodge star convention $*dx_1=dx_2$, $*dx_2=-dx_1$. A field pair is a smooth real one-form $A=A_1dx_1+A_2dx_2$ on the trivial complex line over $\\mathbb R^2$ and a smooth function $q:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb C$. Its unit-charge covariant derivative is $D_Aq=dq-iAq$, its curvature is $F_A=dA=B\\,dx_1\\wedge dx_2$ with $B=\\partial_1A_2-\\partial_2A_1$, and its transverse energy is\n$$E(A,q)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{B^2}{2e^2}+|D_{A,1}q|^2+|D_{A,2}q|^2+\\frac{e^2}{2}(|q|^2-\\xi)^2\\right)dx_1dx_2.$$\nThe pair has finite energy when this nonnegative Lebesgue integral is finite. An admissible boundary sector consists of an integer $n$, a radius $R_0$, and smooth functions $\\rho>0$ and real $2\\pi$-periodic $\\phi$ on $r>R_0$ such that $q(r,\\theta)=\\rho(r,\\theta)e^{i(n\\theta+\\phi(r,\\theta))}$ and $\\rho\\to\\sqrt\\xi$, $\\phi\\to0$ uniformly as $r\\to\\infty$; $n$ is its winding. A gauge transformation is $(q,A)\\mapsto(e^{i\\chi}q,A+d\\chi)$ for a smooth real-valued function $\\chi$ on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, the positive critical first-order equations are $(D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$; for $n\\le0$ the negative equations are $(D_{A,1}-iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=-e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$. The adjective critical refers to the displayed energy normalization and these square-saturating equations. For a positive-winding solution, its divisor is an effective finite divisor $D=\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j[z_j]$, where the $z_j\\in\\mathbb C$ are distinct, the multiplicities $m_j$ are positive integers, $q$ vanishes exactly at the $z_j$, and near each $z_j$ it has the local form $q(z)=(z-z_j)^{m_j}g_j(z)$ with $g_j$ smooth and nonzero there. The degree is $N=\\sum_jm_j$; the empty divisor has degree zero. Negative winding uses the conjugate field and reversed orientation, with $N=|n|$.\n\nOn the complement of the divisor, define $h=\\log(|q|^2/\\xi)$. The associated scalar Taubes datum is a real function smooth away from the divisor such that $h-2m_j\\log|z-z_j|$ extends smoothly across every $z_j$, $h\\to0$ at infinity, and, as a distribution,\n$$\\Delta h+\\kappa(1-e^h)=4\\pi\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j\\delta_{z_j},\\qquad \\langle\\delta_z,\\psi\\rangle=\\psi(z)\\quad(\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)).$$\nWhen the limit exists, the magnetic flux is $\\Phi(A)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{|x|<R}B(x)\\,dx_1dx_2$. These are mathematical configuration and normalization conventions; they make no assertion that a physical model describes nature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qftx-taubes-arbitrary-divisor-plane-vortex",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix $e>0$, $\\xi>0$, $\\kappa=2e^2\\xi$, and an effective finite divisor $D=\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j[z_j]$ on $\\mathbb C$ with distinct centers and positive integer multiplicities; let $N=\\sum_jm_j$. There is a unique real scalar function $h$, smooth on $\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{z_j\\}$, such that $h-2m_j\\log|z-z_j|$ extends smoothly across each center, $h\\to0$ as $|z|\\to\\infty$, and\n$$\\Delta h+\\kappa(1-e^h)=4\\pi\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j\\delta_{z_j}$$\nin distributions, where $\\langle\\delta_z,\\psi\\rangle=\\psi(z)$ for $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$. For $N>0$, $h<0$ off the divisor; for the empty divisor, $h=0$. With $P(z)=\\prod_j(z-z_j)^{m_j}$, $s=2\\log|P|$, and the orientation $*dx_1=dx_2$, $*dx_2=-dx_1$, the fields\n$$q(z)=\\sqrt\\xi\\,e^{(h(z)-s(z))/2}P(z),\\qquad A=-\\tfrac12 *d(h-s)$$\nextend smoothly across the centers and solve $(D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$, where $D_A=d-iA$ and $B\\,dx_1\\wedge dx_2=dA$. They have finite energy\n$$E(A,q)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{B^2}{2e^2}+|D_{A,1}q|^2+|D_{A,2}q|^2+\\frac{e^2}{2}(|q|^2-\\xi)^2\\right)dx_1dx_2$$\nand flux $\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}B\\,dx_1dx_2=2\\pi N$. For every $0<\\alpha<\\sqrt\\kappa$ and every multi-index $\\beta$, $|\\partial^\\beta h(z)|=O(e^{-\\alpha|z|})$ as $|z|\\to\\infty$; the curvature $B$ and covariant gradient $D_Aq$ have the same exponential decay. On the exterior of a disk, the local gauge $e^{-i\\arg P}$ gives $q_{\\mathrm{ext}}=\\sqrt\\xi e^{h/2}$ and $A_{\\mathrm{ext}}=-\\tfrac12 *dh$, so the fields approach the vacuum exponentially in that exterior gauge; the raw global representative $q$ need not have an exponentially small phase correction. Negative winding $-N$ is obtained by complex conjugation and $A\\mapsto-A$, with flux $-2\\pi N$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-general-stokes-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M$ be an oriented smooth $n$-manifold with boundary, $n\\geq1$, and let $\\eta\\in\\Omega_c^{n-1}(M)$. With $j:\\partial M\\hookrightarrow M$ and the outward-normal-first orientation,\n$$\\int_M d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial M}j^*\\eta.$$\nAn empty boundary contributes zero; in dimension one its integral is a finite signed sum of point values.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. Let $f_n:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable for every $n\\ge1$, with $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$, and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$ for every $x\\in X$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f,g\\in\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$, then\n\n$$\\int |fg|\\,d\\mu\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|g\\|_2.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $f,g$ is zero almost everywhere,\nor there is a constant $c>0$ with\n\n$$|f|^2=c\\,|g|^2\\qquad\\mu\\text{-almost everywhere}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Define the real current one-form $j=j_1dx_1+j_2dx_2$ by $j_i=\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline qD_{A,i}q)$. Expanding the squares and using $[D_{A,1},D_{A,2}]q=-iBq$ gives\n$$|D_Aq|^2=|(D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2})q|^2+\\partial_1j_2-\\partial_2j_1+B|q|^2.$$\nAlso, direct expansion gives\n$$\\frac{B^2}{2e^2}+\\frac{e^2}{2}(\\xi-|q|^2)^2=\\frac{(B-e^2(\\xi-|q|^2))^2}{2e^2}+B(\\xi-|q|^2).$$\nAdding these identities yields the positive-winding disk identity with boundary term $\\int_{D_R}(\\partial_1j_2-\\partial_2j_1)$. Repeating the expansion with $D_{A,1}-iD_{A,2}$ and $B+e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$ gives the negative-winding identity with the opposite signs.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For almost every $r$ larger than the nonzero-field radius, let $Q(r)=\\int_{\\partial D_r}(|q|^2-\\xi)^2ds$ and $K(r)=\\int_{\\partial D_r}|D_{A,\\tau}q|^2ds$, where $D_{A,\\tau}$ is the covariant derivative in the unit tangent direction. The potential and kinetic terms of the finite energy, with polar coordinates and [F4], imply $Q,K\\in L^1([R_0,\\infty))$. For each integer $m\\ge1$, the set $S_m=\\{r\\ge R_0:Q(r)>1/m\\}$ has finite measure. Its complement has divergent $\\int dr/r$, since removing a finite-measure set from a half-line cannot make the logarithmic integral finite. If $rK(r)\\ge1/m$ for every sufficiently large $r\\notin S_m$, then $\\int K(r)dr\\ge m^{-1}\\int_{[R,\\infty)\\setminus S_m}dr/r=\\infty$, contradiction. Thus there is an unbounded set of radii with $Q(r)\\le1/m$ and $rK(r)<1/m$. By [A1] select an increasing sequence $r_m\\to\\infty$ from these sets. Along it, [F6] gives $\\int_{\\partial D_{r_m}}|q|^2ds\\le2\\pi\\xi r_m+\\sqrt{2\\pi r_mQ(r_m)}=O(r_m)$, and\n$$\\left|\\int_{\\partial D_{r_m}}j\\right|\\le\\left(\\int_{\\partial D_{r_m}}|q|^2ds\\right)^{1/2}K(r_m)^{1/2}\\longrightarrow0.$$",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For sufficiently large $r$, $|q|\\ge\\sqrt\\xi/2$. On $\\partial D_r$, $j=|q|^2(d(n\\theta+\\phi)-A)$, so $\\int_{\\partial D_r}A=2\\pi n-\\int_{\\partial D_r}j/|q|^2$ because $\\phi$ is single-valued and periodic. Along the sequence from step 1.2,\n$$\\left|\\int_{\\partial D_{r_m}}\\frac{j}{|q|^2}\\right|\\le\\frac{2}{\\sqrt\\xi}(2\\pi r_mK(r_m))^{1/2}\\longrightarrow0.$$\nBy [F3], $\\int_{D_{r_m}}B\\to2\\pi n$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Integrate the positive-winding disk identity from step 1.1 over $D_{r_m}$ and use [F3] on the current term. The two square integrals are nonnegative, while the boundary-current integral tends to zero by step 1.2 and the flux integral tends to $2\\pi n$ by step 2.1. By [F5], the integrals of the nonnegative energy and square densities over the increasing disks converge to their plane integrals, so taking the limit gives the positive-sign exact identity and hence $E(A,q)\\ge2\\pi\\xi n$ for $n\\ge0$. For $n\\le0$, apply the positive-sign identity from step 1.1 to $(\\overline q,-A)$, whose winding is $-n\\ge0$; its energy is unchanged and its curvature changes sign. The resulting identity is exactly the negative-sign identity for $(A,q)$ and gives $E(A,q)\\ge-2\\pi\\xi n$. Thus the bound is $E(A,q)\\ge2\\pi\\xi|n|$, and both identities hold when $n=0$ as well.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "For each fixed divisor, [F2] supplies a smooth pair satisfying the relevant first-order equations. Its square terms in step 1.1 vanish identically; the sequence estimates of steps 1.2–2.1 apply because [F2] gives finite energy and the same boundary sector. Hence its energy is exactly $2\\pi\\xi|n|$. The lower bound of step 3.1 applies to every admissible same-winding configuration, so this pair is an absolute transverse energy minimum, without radial or unit-winding assumptions.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Let $(a,\\eta)$ be a smooth compactly supported variation of a minimizing pair, and set $A_t=A+ta$, $q_t=q+t\\eta$; for any field expression $F$, define $\\delta F:=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_{t=0}F(A_t,q_t)$. For sufficiently small $t$, the fields remain in the same boundary sector; outside the compact support they equal the original fields. Thus the boundary-current limit in the disk identity is unchanged and the flux is unchanged by Stokes applied to $d a$. The energy along this variation is the constant $2\\pi\\xi|n|$ plus the squared $L^2$ norms of the two first-order residuals in step 1.1. Both residuals vanish at $t=0$; differentiating the squared norms gives $E'(0)=0$ and\n$$E''(0)=2\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(|\\delta(D_{A,1}\\pm iD_{A,2})q|^2+\\frac{|\\delta(B\\mp e^2(\\xi-|q|^2))|^2}{2e^2}\\right)dx_1dx_2\\ge0,$$\nwith the upper signs for $n\\ge0$ and lower signs for $n\\le0$ (either choice when $n=0$). Therefore the first variation vanishes and no smooth compactly supported same-sector perturbation lowers the energy. Distinct divisors give distinct possible minimizing fields, so no cross-divisor uniqueness follows.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
