          "source": "def-qftx-critical-abelian-higgs-divisor-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $e>0$ and $\\xi>0$ be real parameters, set $\\kappa=2e^2\\xi$, identify the oriented Euclidean plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with $\\mathbb C$ using coordinates $(x_1,x_2)$, and use the Hodge star convention $*dx_1=dx_2$, $*dx_2=-dx_1$. A field pair is a smooth real one-form $A=A_1dx_1+A_2dx_2$ on the trivial complex line over $\\mathbb R^2$ and a smooth function $q:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb C$. Its unit-charge covariant derivative is $D_Aq=dq-iAq$, its curvature is $F_A=dA=B\\,dx_1\\wedge dx_2$ with $B=\\partial_1A_2-\\partial_2A_1$, and its transverse energy is\n$$E(A,q)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{B^2}{2e^2}+|D_{A,1}q|^2+|D_{A,2}q|^2+\\frac{e^2}{2}(|q|^2-\\xi)^2\\right)dx_1dx_2.$$\nThe pair has finite energy when this nonnegative Lebesgue integral is finite. An admissible boundary sector consists of an integer $n$, a radius $R_0$, and smooth functions $\\rho>0$ and real $2\\pi$-periodic $\\phi$ on $r>R_0$ such that $q(r,\\theta)=\\rho(r,\\theta)e^{i(n\\theta+\\phi(r,\\theta))}$ and $\\rho\\to\\sqrt\\xi$, $\\phi\\to0$ uniformly as $r\\to\\infty$; $n$ is its winding. A gauge transformation is $(q,A)\\mapsto(e^{i\\chi}q,A+d\\chi)$ for a smooth real-valued function $\\chi$ on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, the positive critical first-order equations are $(D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$; for $n\\le0$ the negative equations are $(D_{A,1}-iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=-e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$. The adjective critical refers to the displayed energy normalization and these square-saturating equations. For a positive-winding solution, its divisor is an effective finite divisor $D=\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j[z_j]$, where the $z_j\\in\\mathbb C$ are distinct, the multiplicities $m_j$ are positive integers, $q$ vanishes exactly at the $z_j$, and near each $z_j$ it has the local form $q(z)=(z-z_j)^{m_j}g_j(z)$ with $g_j$ smooth and nonzero there. The degree is $N=\\sum_jm_j$; the empty divisor has degree zero. Negative winding uses the conjugate field and reversed orientation, with $N=|n|$.\n\nOn the complement of the divisor, define $h=\\log(|q|^2/\\xi)$. The associated scalar Taubes datum is a real function smooth away from the divisor such that $h-2m_j\\log|z-z_j|$ extends smoothly across every $z_j$, $h\\to0$ at infinity, and, as a distribution,\n$$\\Delta h+\\kappa(1-e^h)=4\\pi\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j\\delta_{z_j},\\qquad \\langle\\delta_z,\\psi\\rangle=\\psi(z)\\quad(\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)).$$\nWhen the limit exists, the magnetic flux is $\\Phi(A)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{|x|<R}B(x)\\,dx_1dx_2$. These are mathematical configuration and normalization conventions; they make no assertion that a physical model describes nature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qftx-taubes-arbitrary-divisor-plane-vortex",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix $e>0$, $\\xi>0$, $\\kappa=2e^2\\xi$, and an effective finite divisor $D=\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j[z_j]$ on $\\mathbb C$ with distinct centers and positive integer multiplicities; let $N=\\sum_jm_j$. There is a unique real scalar function $h$, smooth on $\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{z_j\\}$, such that $h-2m_j\\log|z-z_j|$ extends smoothly across each center, $h\\to0$ as $|z|\\to\\infty$, and\n$$\\Delta h+\\kappa(1-e^h)=4\\pi\\sum_{j=1}^Jm_j\\delta_{z_j}$$\nin distributions, where $\\langle\\delta_z,\\psi\\rangle=\\psi(z)$ for $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$. For $N>0$, $h<0$ off the divisor; for the empty divisor, $h=0$. With $P(z)=\\prod_j(z-z_j)^{m_j}$, $s=2\\log|P|$, and the orientation $*dx_1=dx_2$, $*dx_2=-dx_1$, the fields\n$$q(z)=\\sqrt\\xi\\,e^{(h(z)-s(z))/2}P(z),\\qquad A=-\\tfrac12 *d(h-s)$$\nextend smoothly across the centers and solve $(D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2})q=0$ and $B=e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$, where $D_A=d-iA$ and $B\\,dx_1\\wedge dx_2=dA$. They have finite energy\n$$E(A,q)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{B^2}{2e^2}+|D_{A,1}q|^2+|D_{A,2}q|^2+\\frac{e^2}{2}(|q|^2-\\xi)^2\\right)dx_1dx_2$$\nand flux $\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}B\\,dx_1dx_2=2\\pi N$. For every $0<\\alpha<\\sqrt\\kappa$ and every multi-index $\\beta$, $|\\partial^\\beta h(z)|=O(e^{-\\alpha|z|})$ as $|z|\\to\\infty$; the curvature $B$ and covariant gradient $D_Aq$ have the same exponential decay. On the exterior of a disk, the local gauge $e^{-i\\arg P}$ gives $q_{\\mathrm{ext}}=\\sqrt\\xi e^{h/2}$ and $A_{\\mathrm{ext}}=-\\tfrac12 *dh$, so the fields approach the vacuum exponentially in that exterior gauge; the raw global representative $q$ need not have an exponentially small phase correction. Negative winding $-N$ is obtained by complex conjugation and $A\\mapsto-A$, with flux $-2\\pi N$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-qftx-critical-vortex-global-energy-minimum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix $e>0$ and $\\xi>0$ and use the critical Abelian-Higgs energy and boundary-sector convention in which $D_A=d-iA$, $B=dA$, $q$ tends in modulus to $\\sqrt\\xi$, and its exterior phase has winding $n\\in\\mathbb Z$. For every smooth finite-energy field pair $(A,q)$ in that sector,\n$$E(A,q)\\ge 2\\pi\\xi|n|.$$\nFor $n\\ge0$, the exact identity is\n$$E(A,q)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(|(D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2})q|^2+\\frac{(B-e^2(\\xi-|q|^2))^2}{2e^2}\\right)dx_1dx_2+2\\pi\\xi n;$$\nfor $n\\le0$, replace $D_{A,1}+iD_{A,2}$ by $D_{A,1}-iD_{A,2}$ and $B-e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$ by $B+e^2(\\xi-|q|^2)$, with topological term $-2\\pi\\xi n$. The boundary-current term in the disk identity vanishes along a radius sequence derived from the finite-energy hypotheses. The fields reconstructed for any fixed divisor by the arbitrary-divisor plane theorem attain equality, are absolute transverse energy minima in their winding sector, have zero first variation under smooth compactly supported variations, and have nonnegative second variation in those directions. This does not assert uniqueness of minimizing fields across distinct divisors of the same degree.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-general-stokes-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M$ be an oriented smooth $n$-manifold with boundary, $n\\geq1$, and let $\\eta\\in\\Omega_c^{n-1}(M)$. With $j:\\partial M\\hookrightarrow M$ and the outward-normal-first orientation,\n$$\\int_M d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial M}j^*\\eta.$$\nAn empty boundary contributes zero; in dimension one its integral is a finite signed sum of point values.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Decompose the integrand into the transverse density $e_\\perp(x,z)$, containing $F_{12}$, $D_1q,D_2q$ and the potential, and the nonnegative longitudinal density $e_\\parallel(x,z)$, containing $F_{31},F_{32},D_3q$. Since the total density is nonnegative and integrable, [F4] gives $\\mathcal E_L=\\int_0^L E(\\mathbf A_\\perp(\\cdot,z),q_z)\\,dz+\\int_{\\Omega_L}e_\\parallel\\,dx\\,dz$, where $\\mathbf A_\\perp=A_1dx_1+A_2dx_2$ and $E(\\mathbf A_\\perp(\\cdot,z),q_z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}e_\\perp(x,z)dx$ for almost every $z$. The slice hypotheses and [F3] give $E(\\mathbf A_\\perp(\\cdot,z),q_z)\\ge T_n$ for each slice, while the longitudinal integral is nonnegative; hence $\\mathcal E_L\\ge T_nL$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For finite $R$, apply [F5] to $D_R\\times\\{z\\}$ to identify its magnetic flux with $\\int_{\\partial D_R}A_\\perp(\\cdot,z)$. Apply [F5] to the side $\\partial D_R\\times[0,L]$: its flux is the difference of the two oriented end-circle integrals. The uniform angular one-form condition makes both integrals $2\\pi n+o(1)$, so the side flux tends to zero. Thus the stated angular condition enforces the required no-leakage condition and is consistent with the fixed slice flux.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "For an end trace with exterior phase $n\\theta+\\phi$, choose a smooth radial cutoff $\\eta$ which is zero on a disk containing the noncanonical region and one outside a larger disk. The real function $\\chi=-\\eta\\phi$ extends smoothly by zero to the whole plane; the allowed gauge transformation $(q,A)\\mapsto(e^{i\\chi}q,A+d\\chi)$ therefore makes the exterior phase exactly $n\\theta$. This constructs the asserted degree-zero exterior phase cleanup without attempting to extend a degree-$n$ angular map across the disk. For the planar minimizer in [F2], write $P=|P|e^{i\\vartheta}$ on the exterior; $\\vartheta-n\\theta$ is single-valued, smooth and tends to zero because $P(z)=z^n(1+O(|z|^{-1}))$ for $n\\ge0$ (use the conjugate construction for $n<0$). Applying the same cutoff extension to $-(\\vartheta-n\\theta)$ changes its exterior fields to $q^*=\\rho_*e^{in\\theta}$ and $A^*=n\\,d\\theta-\\tfrac12*dh$; hence $A^*-n\\,d\\theta$ decays exponentially.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "step": "1.4",
          "claim": "In canonical exterior gauge write $q=\\rho e^{in\\theta}$ and $\\alpha=A-n\\,d\\theta$. Directly, $D_Aq=e^{in\\theta}(d\\rho-i\\rho\\alpha)$, so the real and imaginary terms are orthogonal and $|D_Aq|^2=|d\\rho|^2+\\rho^2|\\alpha|^2$. Uniform convergence gives $\\rho\\ge\\sqrt\\xi/2$ outside a disk; thus finite kinetic energy implies $\\alpha\\in L^2$ there. Also $|\\rho-\\sqrt\\xi|\\le |\\rho^2-\\xi|/\\sqrt\\xi$, so finite potential energy gives $\\rho-\\sqrt\\xi\\in L^2$ there. On compact disks all fields are smooth and bounded. Consequently, for any two cleaned end traces and the cleaned minimizer, differences of connections and Higgs fields are globally in $L^2$; each Higgs field is bounded.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For any two such canonical profiles $p_a=(A^a,q^a)$ and $p_b=(A^b,q^b)$, put $A_t=(1-t)A^a+tA^b$, $q_t=(1-t)q^a+tq^b$, $0\\le t\\le1$, and $\\delta A=A^b-A^a$, $\\delta q=q^b-q^a$. The curvature is $B_t=(1-t)B_a+tB_b\\in L^2$, so linearity of the flux integral gives flux $2\\pi n$ for every blend because both endpoint fluxes equal $2\\pi n$. On the exterior, $q_t=e^{in\\theta}\\rho_t$ with $\\rho_t=(1-t)\\rho_a+t\\rho_b$; the uniform bounds and $\\rho_t-\\sqrt\\xi\\in L^2$ imply $|q_t|^2-\\xi\\in L^2$. Expanding the covariant derivative gives\n$$D_{A_t}q_t=(1-t)D_{A^a}q^a+tD_{A^b}q^b+i\\,t(1-t)\\delta A\\,\\delta q.$$\nThe first two terms are in $L^2$ by finite end energy, and the last is in $L^2$ because $\\delta A\\in L^2$ and $\\delta q$ is bounded. Thus every transverse energy term of the blend is integrable, with a bound uniform for $t\\in[0,1]$. Its exterior phase, vacuum limit and angular one-form limit are preserved by convexity and linearity.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\ell>0$ and a smooth function $\\sigma:[0,1]\\to[0,1]$ which is zero near $0$ and one near $1$. On a collar of width $\\ell$, set $t(z)=\\sigma(z/\\ell)$, $A_1,A_2=A_{t(z)}$, $q=q_{t(z)}$, and $A_3=0$. Then $D_3q=(\\sigma'(z/\\ell)/\\ell)\\delta q$ and $F_{3i}=(\\sigma'(z/\\ell)/\\ell)\\delta A_i$. Their squared integrals are finite by [F1] and step 1.4. Step 2.1 bounds the transverse density uniformly in $z$, so the complete collar energy is a finite constant depending only on its two profiles, $\\ell$, and $\\sigma$. Since $\\sigma$ is constant near each end, this collar joins smoothly to constant-in-$z$ profiles. The angular limit is the convex combination of the endpoint limits; its side flux tends to zero by step 1.2.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Apply step 3.1 first to $p_0$ and $p_*$, defining its finite energy to be $C_0$, and then to $p_*$ and $p_1$, defining its finite energy to be $C_1$. For $L\\ge2\\ell$, place the first collar on $[0,\\ell]$, the constant planar minimizer on $[\\ell,L-\\ell]$, and the second collar on $[L-\\ell,L]$. The profiles match smoothly because the collar cutoff is constant near its ends. The middle has no longitudinal energy and contributes exactly $T_n(L-2\\ell)$ by [F2]–[F3]. At $L=2\\ell$ this middle interval has zero length; the two collars meet at the constant value $p_*$, so the same upper bound holds. This admissible concatenation proves $\\inf\\mathcal E_L\\le C_0+T_n(L-2\\ell)+C_1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Combining steps 1.1 and 4.1 gives $0\\le\\inf\\mathcal E_L-T_nL\\le C_0+C_1-2\\ell T_n$ for every $L\\ge2\\ell$; the right side is a finite constant independent of $L$, proving the bounded-remainder conclusion. If $p_0=p_1=p_*$, the constant extension $(A_1,A_2,q)=(A^*,q^*)$, $A_3=0$, has energy $T_nL$ and satisfies the angular and no-leakage conditions. The lower bound from step 1.1 then proves it is an exact translation-invariant minimum.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The Statement selects the empty divisor for n=0; steps 4.1 and 5.1 give its vacuum cylinder value."
        },
