items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:52:## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:56:[F1] [[def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module]]: a relation germ of $(P_1,\ldots,P_q)$ is a tuple $g=(g_1,\ldots,g_q)$ of holomorphic germs satisfying $\sum_{j=1}^q g_jP_j=0$.
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:58:[F2] [[def-weierstrass-polynomial]]: each $P_j$ is monic in $w$, has a positive finite degree, and has holomorphic coefficients on $U'$.
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:60:[F3] [[thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem]]: a germ regular in $w$ of order $r$ is a unit times a monic Weierstrass polynomial of degree $r$.
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:62:[F4] [[thm-weierstrass-division-theorem]]: division by a monic Weierstrass polynomial has a unique holomorphic quotient and a unique polynomial remainder of smaller degree.
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:64:[F5] [[thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables]] and [[cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients]]: a holomorphic germ has a convergent power series with uniquely determined coefficients; the product of two nonzero germs is nonzero because the product of their first nonzero homogeneous terms is a nonzero polynomial.
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:66:[F6] [[def-regular-holomorphic-germ]]: regularity in $w-w_0$ of order $r$ means the restriction to the $w$-line has a zero of exact finite order $r$.
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:70:1.1 If $q=1$, a relation germ $g$ satisfies $gP_1=0$ by [F1]. The polynomial $P_1$ is nonzero by [F2], and [F5] makes the ring of holomorphic germs an integral domain, so $g=0$, which is already a polynomial relation of bounded degree. [F1, F2, F5, given, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:72:2.1 Suppose $q\ge2$. If $P_q(x)\ne0$, then $P_q$ is a unit germ. For every relation $g=(g_1,\ldots,g_q)$, [F1] gives $g_q=-\sum_{j<q}g_jP_j/P_q$, hence $g=\sum_{j<q}(g_j/P_q)(P_qe_j-P_je_q)$. Each vector $P_qe_j-P_je_q$ is a relation whose component degrees are at most $d$. [F1, F2, given, construct, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:74:3.1 Suppose $P_q(x)=0$, and let $r\ge1$ be its order of vanishing in $w-w_0$; this is finite and at most $d$ by [F2, F6]. By [F3], after shrinking near $x$ write $P_q=u p$, where $u$ is a holomorphic unit and $p$ is a monic Weierstrass polynomial of degree $r$ in $w-w_0$. Ordinary polynomial division of $P_q$ by the monic polynomial $p$ gives a polynomial quotient and a remainder of degree below $r$. The division pair $(u,0)$ also represents $P_q$ as a holomorphic germ divided by $p$; uniqueness in [F4] makes the polynomial remainder zero and identifies the polynomial quotient with $u$. Since $P_q$ and $p$ are monic of degrees $d$ and $r$, respectively, this polynomial $u$ has degree $d-r$ and remains a unit near $x$. [F2, F3, F4, F6, given, construct]
items/lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations.md:76:4.1 Let $g=(g_1,\ldots,g_q)$ be a relation germ at $x$. For $j<q$, divide by $p$ using [F4] to write $g_j=t_jp+r_j$, where $t_j$ is holomorphic and $r_j$ is a polynomial in $w-w_0$ of degree below $r$. Set $\alpha_j=t_j/u$ and $v_j=P_qe_j-P_je_q$. Then $\delta=g-\sum_{j<q}\alpha_jv_j$ is a relation whose first $q-1$ components are $r_j$. Its last component satisfies $p(u\delta_q)=-\sum_{j<q}r_jP_j$, whose right-hand side is a polynomial of degree at most $d+r-1$. Dividing this polynomial by $p$ and using uniqueness [F4] shows that $u\delta_q$ is a polynomial of degree at most $d-1$. Each $ur_j$ also has degree at most $d-1$, since $\deg u=d-r$ and $\deg r_j<r$. Thus $u\delta$ is a polynomial relation with all component degrees at most $d$, and $g=\sum_{j<q}\alpha_jv_j+u^{-1}(u\delta)$ is generated by polynomial relation germs of the required bound. [F1, F2, F4, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
