i|_\\kappa\\to v$ and $n_\\infty(\\omega):=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\Phi(R\\omega)/v$ to exist smoothly on the unit sphere $S^2$ of spatial directions. Here $|X|_\\kappa^2:=\\kappa(X,X)$. The limiting tangential covariant derivative of $n_\\infty$ vanishes, and the limiting magnetic field lies in the pointwise stabilizer Lie algebra $\\{X\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2):[X,n_\\infty(\\omega)]=0\\}$. The stabilizer subgroup of $T_3$ is $U(1)=\\{\\exp(\\theta T_3):\\theta\\in\\mathbb R/(4\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\}$. On closed northern and southern hemispheres choose smooth local lifts $u_N,u_S$ with $\\operatorname{Ad}_{u_\\alpha}T_3=n_\\infty$; their equatorial transition is $u_S=u_N h$ with $h(\\varphi)=\\exp(\\theta(\\varphi)T_3)$, where $\\varphi\\in[0,2\\pi]$ parametrizes the equator and $\\theta:[0,2\\pi]\\to\\mathbb R$ is a smooth lift. The local adapted frames, connection forms, and curvature forms extend smoothly, respectively $C^1$, to open neighborhoods of the closed hemispheres. The surface limit below exists. The admissible external Wilson probes include the fundamental doublet, on which $T_3$ has weights $\\pm\\tfrac12$; this specifies the electric probe lattice without adding a propagating fundamental matter field. Define the classical magnetic surface charge and its energy normalization by\n\n$$m:=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{S_R^2}\\kappa(\\Phi,B_i)\\,\\hat x_i\\,dS,\\qquad T:=g^{-2}m,$$\n\nwhere $\\hat x=x/|x|$ is the outward unit normal and $dS$ is Euclidean area. The transition increment $\\theta(2\\pi)-\\theta(0)$ is not assumed to be quantized in this definition. These are adopted classical model data; they assert no empirical law, quantum continuum construction, supersymmetry representation, or quantum BPS state.",
