{
  "id": "lem-qftx-analytic-coherent-matrix-kernels",
  "facts": [
    {
      "label": "F1",
      "links": [
        "def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module"
      ],
      "text": "[F1] [[def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module]]: coherence of $\\mathcal A$ means that every finite tuple of sections of $\\mathcal A$ has a locally finite-type relation sheaf."
    },
    {
      "label": "F2",
      "links": [
        "def-kernel-cokernel-image-sheaves"
      ],
      "text": "[F2] [[def-kernel-cokernel-image-sheaves]]: the kernel sheaf of a module morphism is the subsheaf whose sections are objectwise kernels."
    },
    {
      "label": "F3",
      "links": [
        "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise"
      ],
      "text": "[F3] [[thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise]]: equality and exactness of the relevant sheaf maps can be checked on every stalk."
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "text": "Induct on the target rank $q$. If $q=0$, then $\\ker\\Phi=\\mathcal A^{\\oplus p}$, generated by its $p$ standard sections (and equal to zero if $p=0$). If $q=1$, the images of the $p$ standard sections are a finite tuple in $\\mathcal A$, and [F1] says its relation sheaf, which is $\\ker\\Phi$ by [F2], is locally of finite type. [F1, F2, given, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "2.1",
      "text": "Assume $q\\ge1$ and the result for target rank $q$. Let $\\Phi:\\mathcal A^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal A^{\\oplus(q+1)}$, and let $\\pi:\\mathcal A^{\\oplus(q+1)}\\to\\mathcal A^{\\oplus q}$ drop the first coordinate. By the induction hypothesis, around any fixed $x\\in X$ the sheaf $\\ker(\\pi\\Phi)$ has finitely many generating sections $s_1,\\ldots,s_r$ whose germs generate every stalk on one neighborhood $V$ of $x$. [step 1.1, given, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "3.1",
      "text": "Since $\\Phi(s_i)$ lies in $\\ker\\pi=\\mathcal A\\oplus0^{\\oplus q}$, write $\\Phi(s_i)=(h_i,0,\\ldots,0)$ with $h_i\\in\\mathcal A(V)$. By [F1], after shrinking $V$ the relation sheaf of $(h_1,\\ldots,h_r)$ is generated on $V$ by finitely many sections $t^{(1)},\\ldots,t^{(m)}\\in\\mathcal A(V)^{\\oplus r}$. For each $\\ell$, define $\\sigma_\\ell=\\sum_{i=1}^r t_i^{(\\ell)}s_i$. Then $\\Phi(\\sigma_\\ell)=0$ because $\\sum_i t_i^{(\\ell)}h_i=0$. [F1, step 2.1, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "4.1",
      "text": "For any $y\\in V$ and any germ $v\\in(\\ker\\Phi)_y$, step 2.1 gives $v=\\sum_i c_i(s_i)_y$ for some germs $c_i\\in\\mathcal A_y$. The first coordinate of $\\Phi_y(v)=0$ says $\\sum_i c_i(h_i)_y=0$, so $(c_i)_i$ lies in the relation stalk generated by the $t^{(\\ell)}_y$. Hence $v$ is an $\\mathcal A_y$-linear combination of $(\\sigma_\\ell)_y$. Thus these finitely many sections generate every kernel stalk on $V$; [F2] and [F3] identify the resulting subsheaf with $\\ker\\Phi$, proving local finite type. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎"
    }
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations",
  "facts": [
    {
      "label": "F1",
      "links": [
        "def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module"
      ],
      "text": "[F1] [[def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module]]: a relation germ of $(P_1,\\ldots,P_q)$ is a tuple $g=(g_1,\\ldots,g_q)$ of holomorphic germs satisfying $\\sum_{j=1}^q g_jP_j=0$."
    },
    {
      "label": "F2",
      "links": [
        "def-weierstrass-polynomial"
      ],
      "text": "[F2] [[def-weierstrass-polynomial]]: each $P_j$ is monic in $w$, has a positive finite degree, and has holomorphic coefficients on $U'$."
    },
    {
      "label": "F3",
      "links": [
        "thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem"
      ],
      "text": "[F3] [[thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem]]: a germ regular in $w$ of order $r$ is a unit times a monic Weierstrass polynomial of degree $r$."
    },
    {
      "label": "F4",
      "links": [
        "thm-weierstrass-division-theorem"
      ],
      "text": "[F4] [[thm-weierstrass-division-theorem]]: division by a monic Weierstrass polynomial has a unique holomorphic quotient and a unique polynomial remainder of smaller degree."
    },
    {
      "label": "F5",
      "links": [
        "thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables",
        "cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients"
      ],
      "text": "[F5] [[thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables]] and [[cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients]]: a holomorphic germ has a convergent power series with uniquely determined coefficients; the product of two nonzero germs is nonzero because the product of their first nonzero homogeneous terms is a nonzero polynomial."
    },
    {
      "label": "F6",
      "links": [
        "def-regular-holomorphic-germ"
      ],
      "text": "[F6] [[def-regular-holomorphic-germ]]: regularity in $w-w_0$ of order $r$ means the restriction to the $w$-line has a zero of exact finite order $r$."
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "text": "If $q=1$, a relation germ $g$ satisfies $gP_1=0$ by [F1]. The polynomial $P_1$ is nonzero by [F2], and [F5] makes the ring of holomorphic germs an integral domain, so $g=0$, which is already a polynomial relation of bounded degree. [F1, F2, F5, given, algebra]"
    },
    {
      "id": "2.1",
      "text": "Suppose $q\\ge2$. If $P_q(x)\\ne0$, then $P_q$ is a unit germ. For every relation $g=(g_1,\\ldots,g_q)$, [F1] gives $g_q=-\\sum_{j<q}g_jP_j/P_q$, hence $g=\\sum_{j<q}(g_j/P_q)(P_qe_j-P_je_q)$. Each vector $P_qe_j-P_je_q$ is a relation whose component degrees are at most $d$. [F1, F2, given, construct, algebra]"
    },
    {
      "id": "3.1",
      "text": "Suppose $P_q(x)=0$, and let $r\\ge1$ be its order of vanishing in $w-w_0$; this is finite and at most $d$ by [F2, F6]. By [F3], after shrinking near $x$ write $P_q=u p$, where $u$ is a holomorphic unit and $p$ is a monic Weierstrass polynomial of degree $r$ in $w-w_0$. Ordinary polynomial division of $P_q$ by the monic polynomial $p$ gives a polynomial quotient and a remainder of degree below $r$. The division pair $(u,0)$ also represents $P_q$ as a holomorphic germ divided by $p$; uniqueness in [F4] makes the polynomial remainder zero and identifies the polynomial quotient with $u$. Since $P_q$ and $p$ are monic of degrees $d$ and $r$, respectively, this polynomial $u$ has degree $d-r$ and remains a unit near $x$. [F2, F3, F4, F6, given, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "4.1",
      "text": "Let $g=(g_1,\\ldots,g_q)$ be a relation germ at $x$. For $j<q$, divide by $p$ using [F4] to write $g_j=t_jp+r_j$, where $t_j$ is holomorphic and $r_j$ is a polynomial in $w-w_0$ of degree below $r$. Set $\\alpha_j=t_j/u$ and $v_j=P_qe_j-P_je_q$. Then $\\delta=g-\\sum_{j<q}\\alpha_jv_j$ is a relation whose first $q-1$ components are $r_j$. Its last component satisfies $p(u\\delta_q)=-\\sum_{j<q}r_jP_j$, whose right-hand side is a polynomial of degree at most $d+r-1$. Dividing this polynomial by $p$ and using uniqueness [F4] shows that $u\\delta_q$ is a polynomial of degree at most $d-1$. Each $ur_j$ also has degree at most $d-1$, since $\\deg u=d-r$ and $\\deg r_j<r$. Thus $u\\delta$ is a polynomial relation with all component degrees at most $d$, and $g=\\sum_{j<q}\\alpha_jv_j+u^{-1}(u\\delta)$ is generated by polynomial relation germs of the required bound. [F1, F2, F4, step 3.1, algebra] ∎"
    }
  ]
}
{
  "id": "lem-qftx-coherent-analytic-module-calculus",
  "facts": [
    {
      "label": "F1",
      "links": [
        "def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module"
      ],
      "text": "[F1] [[def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module]]: a coherent module is locally of finite type, and the kernel of every morphism from a finite-rank free module sheaf into it is locally of finite type."
    },
    {
      "label": "F2",
      "links": [
        "def-kernel-cokernel-image-sheaves"
      ],
      "text": "[F2] [[def-kernel-cokernel-image-sheaves]]: kernels are objectwise; cokernel and image sheaves are the sheafifications of their sectionwise presheaves."
    },
    {
      "label": "F3",
      "links": [
        "def-exact-sequence-sheaves"
      ],
      "text": "[F3] [[def-exact-sequence-sheaves]]: a short exact sequence has zero kernel at the left endpoint, image equal to kernel at the middle term, and zero cokernel at the right endpoint."
    },
    {
      "label": "F4",
      "links": [
        "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise"
      ],
      "text": "[F4] [[thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise]]: epimorphisms and exactness in the constructions below can be checked on stalks."
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "text": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be coherent and fix $x\\in X$. Local finite type gives, after choosing a neighborhood $U$ of $x$, a finite-rank free module sheaf map $\\pi:\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal F|_U$ that is surjective on every stalk. By [F1], its kernel is locally of finite type; after shrinking to $V\\subseteq U$, choose finitely many sections generating that kernel on $V$ and let $\\rho:\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus m}\\to\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus k}$ be the corresponding map. The sequence $\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus m}\\xrightarrow{\\rho}\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus k}\\xrightarrow{\\pi}\\mathcal F|_V\\to0$ is exact by [F2], [F3], and [F4]. Thus every coherent module has a local finite presentation. [F1, F2, F3, F4, given, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "2.1",
      "text": "Let $f:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal G$ be a morphism of coherent modules and set $\\mathcal K=\\ker f$. Choose locally a finite-rank free epimorphism $\\pi:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal F$. The composite $f\\pi:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal G$ has a locally finite-type kernel by [F1]; its image under $\\pi$ is exactly $\\mathcal K$ on each stalk, since $\\pi$ is stalkwise surjective. Hence $\\mathcal K$ is locally of finite type by [F4]. For every finite-rank map $\\eta:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal K$, its composite with the inclusion $\\mathcal K\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal F$ has the same kernel; [F1] for $\\mathcal F$ makes that kernel locally of finite type. Thus $\\mathcal K$ is coherent. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, given]"
    },
    {
      "id": "3.1",
      "text": "Let $\\mathcal C=\\operatorname{coker}f$. It is locally of finite type because it is a quotient of the locally finite-type module $\\mathcal G$. To check its relation condition, fix a finite-rank map $\\gamma:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal C$ and a point $x$. Since $\\mathcal G\\to\\mathcal C$ is a sheaf epimorphism, choose local lifts of the finitely many images of the standard basis near $x$; they define $\\widetilde\\gamma:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal G$ lifting $\\gamma$. Also choose a local finite-rank epimorphism $\\delta:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus m}\\to\\mathcal F$. Define $\\epsilon:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus(m+p)}\\to\\mathcal G$ by $\\epsilon(a,b)=f(\\delta(a))+\\widetilde\\gamma(b)$. By [F1], $\\ker\\epsilon$ is locally of finite type. Projection of $\\ker\\epsilon$ to the second factor has image exactly $\\ker\\gamma$ on every stalk: an element of $\\ker\\epsilon$ maps to zero in $\\mathcal C$, and any stalk element $b\\in\\ker\\gamma$ has $\\widetilde\\gamma(b)\\in\\operatorname{im}f$, so a stalk preimage under $\\delta$ completes it to an element of $\\ker\\epsilon$. The image of a locally finite-type module is locally finite type, so $\\ker\\gamma$ is locally of finite type. This holds for every $\\gamma$, hence $\\mathcal C$ is coherent. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "4.1",
      "text": "Consider a short exact sequence $0\\to\\mathcal F'\\xrightarrow{i}\\mathcal E\\xrightarrow{p}\\mathcal F''\\to0$. If $\\mathcal E$ and $\\mathcal F''$ are coherent, then $\\mathcal F'=\\ker p$ is coherent by step 2.1. If $\\mathcal F'$ and $\\mathcal E$ are coherent, then $\\mathcal F''=\\operatorname{coker}i$ is coherent by step 3.1. [F2, F3, step 2.1, step 3.1]"
    },
    {
      "id": "4.2",
      "text": "It remains to assume $\\mathcal F'$ and $\\mathcal F''$ coherent. Local finite generators of $\\mathcal F'$ together with local lifts to $\\mathcal E$ of finite generators of $\\mathcal F''$ generate $\\mathcal E$, so $\\mathcal E$ is locally of finite type. For any finite-rank map $g:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal E$, the kernel of $pg$ is locally of finite type by [F1] for $\\mathcal F''$; choose a local finite-rank epimorphism $\\gamma:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus m}\\to\\ker(pg)$. Exactness gives a map $\\delta:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus m}\\to\\mathcal F'$ with $i\\delta=g\\gamma$. By [F1] for $\\mathcal F'$, $\\ker\\delta$ is locally of finite type. On each stalk, $\\gamma$ maps $\\ker\\delta$ onto $\\ker g$: inclusion follows from $i\\delta=g\\gamma$, and if $s\\in\\ker g$, then $pg(s)=0$, so $s=\\gamma(t)$ for some $t$ and $i\\delta(t)=g(s)=0$; injectivity of $i$ gives $\\delta(t)=0$. Therefore $\\ker g$ is locally of finite type. Since this holds for every $g$, $\\mathcal E$ is coherent. [F1, F2, F3, F4, step 4.1, algebra, construct] ∎"
    }
  ]
}
{
  "id": "thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-oka-coherence",
  "facts": [
    {
      "label": "A1",
      "links": [
        "def-axiom-of-choice"
      ],
      "text": "[A1] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: every family of nonempty sets has a choice function. AC is the adopted ambient assumption; the proof below makes only finite local selections."
    },
    {
      "label": "F1",
      "links": [
        "def-complex-manifolds-and-holomorphic-vector-bundles"
      ],
      "text": "[F1] [[def-complex-manifolds-and-holomorphic-vector-bundles]]: each point of $M$ has a holomorphic chart onto an open subset of $\\mathbb C^n$, and $\\mathcal O_M$ is the sheaf of holomorphic functions in those charts."
    },
