      "text": "[F2] [[def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module]]: coherence of $\\mathcal O_M$ is local finite generation of every finite tuple's relation sheaf; a coherent module is locally of finite type and has locally finite-type kernels for all finite-rank free maps into it."
    },
    {
      "label": "F13",
      "links": [
        "def-stalk-of-presheaf"
      ],
      "text": "[F13] [[def-stalk-of-presheaf]]: the stalk at a point consists of germs of sections on neighborhoods of that point."
    },
    {
      "label": "F3",
      "links": [
        "lem-qftx-analytic-coherent-matrix-kernels"
      ],
      "text": "[F3] [[lem-qftx-analytic-coherent-matrix-kernels]]: when a structure sheaf is coherent, kernels of finite matrices between finite-rank free module sheaves are locally of finite type."
    },
    {
      "label": "F4",
      "links": [
        "lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations"
      ],
      "text": "[F4] [[lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations]]: for a tuple of monic Weierstrass polynomials of maximum degree $d$, each relation germ is generated by polynomial relation germs of degree at most $d$ at the chosen point."
    },
    {
      "label": "F5",
      "links": [
        "lem-qftx-coherent-analytic-module-calculus"
      ],
      "text": "[F5] [[lem-qftx-coherent-analytic-module-calculus]]: kernels, cokernels, and finite extensions of coherent module sheaves are coherent."
    },
    {
      "label": "F6",
      "links": [
        "thm-holomorphic-germ-ring-is-noetherian"
      ],
      "text": "[F6] [[thm-holomorphic-germ-ring-is-noetherian]]: for each $m\\ge1$, the ring $\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ of holomorphic germs at the origin of $\\mathbb C^m$ is Noetherian."
    },
    {
      "label": "F7",
      "links": [
        "thm-finite-generation-and-finite-presentation-over-a-noetherian-ring"
      ],
      "text": "[F7] [[thm-finite-generation-and-finite-presentation-over-a-noetherian-ring]]: over a Noetherian commutative ring, every finitely generated module is a Noetherian module, so each of its submodules is finitely generated."
    },
    {
      "label": "F8",
      "links": [
        "lem-generic-linear-coordinate-makes-a-holomorphic-germ-regular"
      ],
      "text": "[F8] [[lem-generic-linear-coordinate-makes-a-holomorphic-germ-regular]]: each nonzero holomorphic germ becomes regular in the last variable after an invertible complex-linear coordinate change."
    },
    {
      "label": "F9",
      "links": [
        "def-regular-holomorphic-germ",
        "thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem"
      ],
      "text": "[F9] [[def-regular-holomorphic-germ]] and [[thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem]]: regularity means a finite exact zero order on the last coordinate line, and preparation writes such a germ as a unit times a monic Weierstrass polynomial of that degree."
    },
    {
      "label": "F10",
      "links": [
        "def-weierstrass-polynomial",
        "thm-weierstrass-division-theorem"
      ],
      "text": "[F10] [[def-weierstrass-polynomial]] and [[thm-weierstrass-division-theorem]]: a Weierstrass polynomial is monic of its stated degree with lower coefficients vanishing at the center, and division has unique quotient and lower-degree polynomial remainder."
    },
    {
      "label": "F11",
      "links": [
        "thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables",
        "cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients"
      ],
      "text": "[F11] [[thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables]] and [[cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients]]: nonzero holomorphic germs have nonzero first homogeneous terms, whose product is nonzero in the polynomial ring over $\\mathbb C$."
    },
    {
      "label": "F12",
      "links": [
        "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise"
      ],
      "text": "[F12] [[thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise]]: equality of the constructed relation and kernel sheaves, and exactness of the local presentations, follow from their verified stalk sequences."
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "text": "Work under [A1]. In a holomorphic chart, prove the relation assertion by induction on dimension $n$, and for a fixed $n$ by induction on tuple length $q$. On $\\mathbb C^0=\\{0\\}$ each relation space is a subspace of the finite-dimensional vector space $\\mathbb C^q$, so a finite basis gives local generators. For any dimension, the empty tuple has zero relation sheaf. [A1, F1, F2, given, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "1.2",
      "text": "If some tuple entry has zero germ at the chosen point, [F13] gives a neighborhood on which that entry vanishes identically; its coordinate in a relation is then arbitrary, and the relation sheaf is the direct sum of one copy of $\\mathcal O$ with the shorter tuple's relation sheaf. The induction on $q$ gives finite local generators, and repeating this finite reduction removes every zero germ. If some remaining entry, say $f_1$, is nonzero at the point, shrink until $f_1$ is a unit; the sections $e_j-(f_j/f_1)e_1$, $2\\le j\\le q$, generate the relation sheaf because the relation equation uniquely determines its first coordinate. [F2, F13, step 1.1, construct, algebra]"
    },
    {
      "id": "1.3",
      "text": "It remains to treat a tuple of nonzero germs all vanishing at the point. By [F11], the product $G=\\prod_j f_j$ is a nonzero germ. Apply [F8] to $G$; after the resulting linear coordinate change the restriction of $G$ to the last coordinate line has finite order. Since this restriction is the product of the corresponding one-variable restrictions of the $f_j$, each factor is nonzero and hence has a finite order; each $f_j$ is therefore regular in the last variable. [F8, F11, step 1.2, construct, algebra]"
    },
    {
      "id": "1.4",
      "text": "Apply [F9] to each of the finitely many $f_j$ and intersect the resulting neighborhoods. On one common product polydisc each $f_j=u_jP_j$, where $u_j$ is a unit and $P_j$ is a monic Weierstrass polynomial. Multiplication of the relation coefficients by the units $u_j$ is an isomorphism from the relation sheaf of $(f_j)$ to that of $(P_j)$. Set $d=\\max_j\\deg_w P_j$. [F9, step 1.3, construct, algebra]"
    },
    {
      "id": "2.1",
      "text": "By [F4], whose quotient/remainder step uses the uniqueness in [F10], every relation germ of $(P_1,\\ldots,P_q)$ is generated by polynomial relation germs whose components have $w$-degree at most $d$. Such vectors are identified with coefficient vectors in $\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}^{\\oplus q(d+1)}$, where $\\Delta'$ is the base polydisc. Multiplication by the $P_j$ defines a finite matrix map $\\Psi:\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}^{\\oplus q(d+1)}\\to\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}^{\\oplus(2d+1)}$ by taking coefficients of $\\sum_j Q_jP_j$. Its kernel is exactly the bounded-degree polynomial relation sheaf. [F2, F4, F10, step 1.4, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "2.2",
      "text": "By dimension induction, $\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}$ is coherent; [F3] therefore makes $\\mathcal K=\\ker\\Psi$ locally of finite type. At the base point, its stalk is a submodule of $A^{\\oplus q(d+1)}$, where $A=\\mathcal O_{n-1,0}$ after translation. If $n>1$, [F6] makes $A$ Noetherian; if $n=1$, $A=\\mathbb C$ is a field and hence Noetherian. The finite free module is finitely generated, so [F7] makes it Noetherian and its submodule $\\mathcal K_0$ finitely generated. [F3, F6, F7, step 2.1, given]"
    },
    {
      "id": "2.3",
      "text": "The sheaf $\\mathcal K$ is coherent: it is locally of finite type by step 2.2, and for any finite free map into $\\mathcal K$, composition with its inclusion into the finite free target of $\\Psi$ has the same kernel, locally of finite type by [F3]. A finite-rank free module over the coherent structure sheaf is coherent, since its standard basis gives finite type and [F3] gives the kernel condition. Choose finitely many germs generating $\\mathcal K_0$ and represent them by sections on one smaller base neighborhood. Their cokernel in $\\mathcal K$ is coherent by [F5]; its stalk at the base point is zero by the chosen generators and the stalk definition [F13]. Since that cokernel is locally of finite type, a finite generating family has zero germs there and vanishes after a common shrinking. Thus the selected sections generate every kernel stalk on a smaller base neighborhood. [F2, F3, F5, F12, F13, step 2.2, choose]"
    },
    {
      "id": "2.4",
      "text": "Regard these finite coefficient sections as polynomial relation sections on the product polydisc. Together with the finitely many elementary polynomial relations $P_qe_j-P_je_q$, they generate every bounded-degree relation stalk over the smaller base neighborhood; [F4] then makes them generate every relation stalk of $(P_1,\\ldots,P_q)$ throughout a smaller product neighborhood. The unit isomorphisms from step 1.4 transfer these generators back to $(f_1,\\ldots,f_q)$. Equality of the generated subsheaf with the relation sheaf follows from their equal stalks by [F12]. Thus the relation sheaf is locally of finite type at the chosen point. [F3, F4, F12, step 1.4, step 2.3, algebra]"
    },
    {
      "id": "3.1",
      "text": "Since the point and tuple were arbitrary, every finite tuple of holomorphic functions on every chart has a locally finite-type relation sheaf. A holomorphic coordinate change induces an isomorphism of structure sheaves and transports the relation map and its kernel, so the chart-local property is independent of coordinates. Hence [F2] makes $\\mathcal O_M$ coherent on all of $M$. [F1, F2, F12, step 2.4, given]"
    },
    {
      "id": "3.2",
      "text": "Apply [F5] to the coherent structure sheaf $\\mathcal O_M$. It gives closure of coherent $\\mathcal O_M$-modules under kernels, cokernels, and finite extensions, as stated. [F5, step 3.1, given]"
    },
    {
      "id": "4.1",
      "text": "Fix coherent $\\mathcal F$, $x\\in M$, and finite $\\ell\\ge0$. Local finite type gives a finite-rank epimorphism $\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p_0}\\to\\mathcal F$ near $x$. The finite free source is coherent by [F3] and the coherence of $\\mathcal O_M$ from step 3.1; hence its kernel is coherent by [F5]. Choose a finite-rank epimorphism onto that kernel, compose with its inclusion into $\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p_0}$, and repeat this operation until $\\ell+1$ free modules have been chosen. At each intermediate stage the kernel is coherent by [F5], so the next finite-rank epimorphism exists locally. [F2, F3, F5, step 3.1, given, construct]"
    },
    {
      "id": "4.2",
      "text": "Only finitely many neighborhoods are used for the finite length $\\ell+1$ construction; their intersection is a chart neighborhood $W$ of $x$. Each new free module maps onto the preceding kernel, so the resulting sequence is exact at $\\mathcal F|_W$ and every free term before the leftmost one. No further kernel is chosen at the leftmost term, and no global choices of generators or global resolution are asserted. [F4, F12, step 4.1, algebra] ∎"
    }
  ]
}
